WEBVTT

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.423
For this project, I used ChatGPT to assist with research, coding, financial

00:00:04.423 --> 00:00:05.880
calculations and validation.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.880
The analysis and investment decisions are my own.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.920
The project is for educational purposes and is not investment advice.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.212
My recommendation to the Investment Committee is to pilot both investment

00:00:16.212 --> 00:00:20.386
strategies through paper trading with no real capital allocated at this

00:00:20.386 --> 00:00:20.680
time.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.702
My intended investors are college students and young adults with a flexible

00:00:25.702 --> 00:00:27.040
five-year investment horizon.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:33.213
I established a minimum estimated annual net return of 6%, maximum estimated

00:00:33.213 --> 00:00:38.980
annual volatility 15%, minimum BIL allocation of 5%, and maximum annual

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:40.280
turnover of 25%.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:46.304
These requirements reflect my preference for steady growth while controlling

00:00:46.304 --> 00:00:46.640
risk.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:54.687
My benchmark consists of 50% VOO, 40% BND, and 10% BIL, producing an estimated net

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:56.480
annual return of 6.68%.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.880
With 9.58% volatility.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:06.640
The minimum-volatility portfolio produces an estimated net return of 6.28%.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.776
With 7.35% volatility, the maximum-Sharpe portfolio produces a higher

00:01:11.776 --> 00:01:17.359
estimated net return of 12.24%, but also has a higher volatility of 14.18%.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:22.040
Both optimized portfolios meet all four numerical investment requirements.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:31.120
The minimum-volatility portfolio consists of 40% VOO, 35% BIL, and 25% BND.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:39.569
The maximum-Sharpe portfolio allocates 50% to VOO, 15% to SOXX, 10% to SCHD,

00:01:39.569 --> 00:01:42.400
10% to BIL, and 15% to BND.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:48.437
These strategies provide alternative simulated allocations ranging from 40%

00:01:48.437 --> 00:01:49.920
to 75% equity exposure.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.127
The maximum-Sharpe portfolio offers greater estimated growth potential but

00:01:56.127 --> 00:01:59.240
operates much closer to my volatility ceiling.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:05.429
To validate the reliability of these strategies, I tested changes in

00:02:05.429 --> 00:02:10.698
estimated returns, trading costs, historical estimation windows, and

00:02:10.698 --> 00:02:12.480
investment constraints.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.633
These tests demonstrate how changes in financial assumptions can affect

00:02:16.633 --> 00:02:20.266
portfolio performance and whether the strategies continue to meet my

00:02:20.266 --> 00:02:20.960
requirements.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:29.000
One major concern is SOXX's estimated annual return of approximately 29.92%,

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:34.320
which is based on historical performance and may not continue in the future.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:39.927
In my separate changed-input experiment, reducing this estimate by 10

00:02:39.927 --> 00:02:45.006
percentage points lowered the maximum-Sharpe portfolio's estimated net

00:02:45.006 --> 00:02:46.240
return to 10.03%.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:52.372
demonstrating its sensitivity to individual asset assumptions, the

00:02:52.372 --> 00:02:58.968
minimum-volatility portfolio experienced a historical maximum month-end

00:02:58.968 --> 00:03:05.937
drawdown of approximately 13.15%, compared to 22.15% for the maximum-Sharpe

00:03:05.937 --> 00:03:09.560
portfolio and 17.81% for the benchmark.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.262
This demonstrates that meeting a 15% estimated volatility requirement does

00:03:15.262 --> 00:03:18.320
not guarantee losses will remain below 15%.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.834
These historical results are useful diagnostics rather than proof of future

00:03:22.834 --> 00:03:23.480
performance.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.723
I would recommend a 12-month paper-trading pilot monitoring both portfolios

00:03:32.723 --> 00:03:35.480
against the benchmark on a monthly basis.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.018
I would monitor realized returns, volatility, drawdown, trading costs, and

00:03:41.018 --> 00:03:41.560
turnover.

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.391
If the portfolio experiences a 15% drawdown, the VOO momentum signal becomes

00:03:46.391 --> 00:03:50.582
nonpositive, the data becomes outdated, or the model cannot identify a

00:03:50.582 --> 00:03:51.720
feasible portfolio.

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:56.560
I would pause new risk-taking and reassess the strategy.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:04.102
Ultimately, both portfolios demonstrated potential, but historical estimates

00:04:04.102 --> 00:04:08.920
are not sufficient evidence to justify investing real money.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.248
I'm requesting that the investment committee approve a 12-month

00:04:12.248 --> 00:04:16.063
paper-trading pilot with 0 percent of real capital allocated until further

00:04:16.063 --> 00:04:18.280
evidence supports adopting either strategy.

